WEBVTT

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:09.870
Claude and ChatGPT write one word at a time. Pick a likely next word, write it down, go again. And you can do exactly that, by hand.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:16.590
Take a picture book. Read it two words at a time, and count which word follows which.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:27.370
Then write something new. Take the last word, look at what came after it in the book, and draw the next word by those counts. More counts, more likely.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:40.210
You can count in tally marks, or marks on a ledger. Draw with a die, a cup of counters or a pile of paper slips. It's the same model every time: just numbers.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.610
And here's everything this little model could have written.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:54.550
A real model isn't a tiny grid. It looks at far more than one word, and learns from trillions of them. But it's the same loop.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:09.950
And it's not new. In 1913 Andrey Markov went through a Russian poem by hand, counting whether a vowel or a consonant came next. Claude Shannon did it with words in 1948. We're just letting everyone else have a go.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:24.310
LLMs Unplugged has the lessons, the slides, the printouts and a short video for every step. And the tools that made them: paste in any text you like, and out come the grids, the sheets and the booklets for it.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:33.970
It's all free, under a Creative Commons licence, at llmsunplugged.org. Grab a picture book, and come and see how it works.
